{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012EW68nVzwks9bAGKaQsHG7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what task to propose next.\n\n## Project State Analysis\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using Unix utilities as a test bed. The goal is to derive formal specs from C source, generate Go implementations, and verify via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n\n- The working branch currently has no code, though implementations exist at generation-merged tags. The first release (rel01.0) is in progress with a single use case focused on the `ts` utility and differential testing. I'm seeing an open issue for implementing the differential testing harness that covers the core requirements.\n\nSince that issue already exists, I shouldn't create a duplicate. The next logical step is implementing the command-line tool itself, which traces back to the product requirements for `ts` implementation. Let me check what constraints apply to proposing this next task. I can only propose one task, and since pkg/testutils already has an open issue, I should propose cmd/ts implementation instead. The constitution requires splitting implementation and tests, so I'll focus on the implementation first, which covers the core requirements: default behavior with line reading and timestamp formatting, custom format strings with subsecond extensions, incremental mode, and elapsed-since-start tracking.\n\nThe implementation also needs to handle the monotonic clock mode, relative-time conversion, exit codes and error handling, and timezone environment variables—all of which would go into R1-R8. The differential testing requirement (R9) would be a separate task that depends on the TimestampNormalizer from pkg/testutils. Now I'm estimating the scope for cmd/ts, which involves main.go with flag parsing and the stdin reading loop...\n\nThe strftime implementation in Go requires format conversion, subsecond extensions, and the different operational modes. The -r mode with regex-based timestamp replacement and relative age calculation is particularly complex and might push the total beyond 350 lines. Given the 250-line constraint, I should probably split the implementation into the core functionality (R1-R5, R7-R8) as one task, then handle the -r mode separately.\n\nSince I can only propose one task, the core cmd/ts implementation is the most critical piece for the initial release. Once the testutils package is complete, I'll focus on getting the main timestamp formatting and mode handling working in cmd/ts/main.go.\n\nThe main technical challenges are converting strftime format codes to Go's time.Format syntax, handling the custom subsecond extensions, implementing the relative time conversion mode with its regex patterns, and managing timezone handling since Go doesn't have a straightforward way to use the TZ environment variable.\n\nGiven the 150-250 line budget, I'm deciding whether to include the -r mode (relative time conversion) in this task or defer it. The -r mode alone would need regex pattern matching, timestamp parsing, and age calculation—probably 50-100 lines on top of the core 150-200 lines for the main functionality. I'm leaning toward including everything in one task and targeting around 250 lines total, since this is my only implementation task and I want to deliver the complete feature set. The -r mode is going to be the most complex part since it needs to parse relative time expressions and convert them to absolute timestamps, but I can handle that within the line budget by using a focused parsing approach rather than a full expression evaluator. I need to map strftime specifiers to Go time tokens, handle subsecond precision atomically with nanosecond accuracy, use GMT internally for elapsed-time modes, detect four timestamp patterns via regex to calculate relative age, and ensure SIGPIPE doesn't cause a non-zero exit by catching write errors in the main loop.\n\nBut I'm realizing the PRD requires proper SIGPIPE handling, and while pkg/sys isn't implemented yet, the dependency map confirms ts doesn't use it anyway. Looking at the actual exit code logic, ts should exit 0 on success or EOF, and non-zero only for bad options or missing dependencies—so I need to verify what prd004-ts R1.6 actually specifies about this behavior.\n\nThe Perl implementation handles SIGPIPE through default behavior, so in Go I'd need to either ignore the signal directly or catch the write error and handle it gracefully. Since ts operates independently without pkg/sys, I'll use the standard library's signal handling to ignore SIGPIPE and ensure we don't exit non-zero when stdout closes unexpectedly.\n\nNow I'm looking at the task structure for implementing the ts command with all its timestamp modes and test coverage requirements. Now I need to handle the -i flag for incremental mode, where each timestamp shows elapsed time since the previous line, with the first line showing time since startup. I'll also implement -s for elapsed-since-start mode showing monotonically increasing elapsed time, and -m for using the monotonic clock. Both -i and -s need to default to '%H:%M:%S' with GMT timezone, and they should be mutually exclusive with an error if both are specified.\n\nFor -r mode, I'm adding relative-time conversion that scans input lines for various timestamp patterns like syslog, ISO-8601, RFC 2822, and lastlog formats, then replaces them with human-readable relative ages like \"15m5s ago\". If a format argument is also provided, I'll convert the matched timestamps to that format instead. Lines without timestamps pass through unchanged, and -r conflicts with -i and -s.\n\nFor error handling, I need to exit cleanly on EOF, return non-zero with usage info to stderr for unrecognized flags, and handle SIGPIPE gracefully so piping to commands like head doesn't cause a non-zero exit.\n\nThe TZ environment variable should control the timezone for wall-clock modes using time.LoadLocation, while -i and -s modes always use UTC for elapsed-time calculations regardless of the user's timezone setting.\n\nI'm also mapping strftime percent-specifiers to Go's time.Format syntax... For the -i and -s flags, I need to convert elapsed time by treating the duration as seconds since epoch in UTC, then formatting it with the same strftime logic. The -r mode computes how long ago a timestamp was and formats it as a human-readable relative string like \"2h 30m 15s ago\", using precompiled regex patterns for efficiency. Finally, I need to handle SIGPIPE by ignoring it at startup and treating write failures to stdout as silent exits, matching GNU behavior without adding any new dependencies. I'm checking the remaining acceptance criteria for the ts tool. The -i and -s flags together should produce an error, the -r flag should parse syslog timestamps and replace them with relative ages, and it should handle lines without timestamps gracefully. The -z flag is also invalid and should error out, and empty input should produce no output.\n\nNow I'm estimating the line count for the Go implementation. The main components are flag parsing, the strftime conversion function, subsecond handling, the stdin loop, and the various mode implementations. The -r mode with its regex patterns and relative age formatting is the most complex piece. I'm looking at roughly 270 lines total, which is a bit over the 250-line target, but the -r mode's complexity justifies that.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the scope. Since -r mode is optional for the MVP and the acceptance criteria only require the default, -s, and -i modes to pass the differential test, I could defer -r mode to a future task and keep this one focused on R1-R5 and R7-R8. That would bring it down to around 200 lines and fit comfortably within the constraints.\n\nLet me break down the line estimates without -r mode: package setup and imports take about 15 lines, flag parsing around 30, the strftime-to-Go conversion logic roughly 40, subsecond extension handling about 25, the main stdin loop around 25, then -i mode, -s mode, -m mode, timezone handling, and error/signal handling each taking their respective shares. That totals roughly 200 lines, which is right in the target range.\n\nI'll revise the task to exclude R6 and note that -r mode is deferred.\n\nLooking at the test suite, there are 13 test cases including some that trace to R6 like the relative mode tests, but those might not be essential for the initial implementation—I can defer them and focus on getting the core acceptance criteria met first.\n\nThe acceptance criteria for rel01.0-uc001-ts requires the differential tests for default, -s, and -i modes to pass, plus handling empty stdin and ensuring all unit tests pass. Since the constitution says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, this task is just the implementation itself, and the actual test verification would happen in a follow-up task. Given that constraint, I should implement the full -r mode since it's part of the complete ts specification, but I need to watch the line count to stay reasonable. the task should focus on the cmd/ts implementation with R1-R5 and R7-R8 to maximize impact for rel01.0, deferring R6 (-r mode) to a future session. I'm finalizing the task definition with the core requirements, dependencies, and necessary documentation references.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific requirements for each mode: the default timestamp mode needs to read stdin line-by-line, prepend formatted timestamps, handle partial lines, and manage EOF and SIGPIPE gracefully. The custom format support requires accepting a strftime format string argument and converting it appropriately. for elapsed formatting to match strftime(fmt, gmtime(delta)) behavior, and I need to reject -i with -s by printing usage to stderr and exiting with a non-zero code. Then there's -s for elapsed-since-start mode showing monotonically increasing time since ts started, defaulting to '%H:%M:%S' with UTC-based elapsed formatting, plus -m for monotonic clock mode that uses time.Now()'s monotonic reading across all timestamp modes and custom formats including subsecond extensions. Finally, I'm implementing exit codes and error handling...\n\nDefining the design approach... for the current timestamp format. The binary must handle multiple input lines correctly, prefixing each with its own timestamp.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"When run with -i flag and input '1 2 3', output is '0.000000 1\\\\n0.000000 2\\\\n0.000000 3\\\\n'. When run with -s flag and same input, output is '0.000000 1\\\\n1.000000 2\\\\n2.000000 3\\\\n'. Elapsed times are formatted as HH:MM:SS.ffffff.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"SIGPIPE handling: when piping to 'head -n 1', the program exits cleanly without error output after writing the first line.\"\n\nFor SIGPIPE handling, I need to ignore the signal at startup and check for broken pipe errors after each write, exiting cleanly without printing anything if the pipe breaks. The implementation lives in the main package under cmd/ts/, and the module is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils. The build must pass golangci-lint checks, and the acceptance criteria cover basic functionality like timestamping multiple lines, handling the -i and -s flags with proper elapsed-time formatting, and gracefully handling SIGPIPE when output is piped to commands like head. I'm finishing up the acceptance criteria with a couple more edge cases—handling input without a trailing newline and testing custom format strings with microsecond precision. Now I need to map these test cases back to the PRD requirements to make sure I'm covering everything systematically, accounting for the fact that I'm excluding the -r mode requirement. I need to reconcile these constraints carefully. The planning constitution requires each PRD requirement to map to one task, but I'm also limited to a single task with a 250-line production code budget. Since -r mode is a distinct feature, I'll defer R6 and scope this task to cover the other requirements within the line limit, making sure the title reflects exactly what's being implemented.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73796,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73796},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f42cd5a-1743-418a-a045-f5a4dc0bd662","uuid":"add11b14-d40c-4feb-abb6-70f1132ebcbd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012EW68nVzwks9bAGKaQsHG7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils derives formal specs from Unix utility C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity via differential testing against Homebrew GNU binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: `pkg/testutils` (differential harness) → `pkg/sys` (syscall abstractions) → `pkg/format` (output formatting) → `cmd/<utility>/` (implementations). Human-guided spec phase, automated synthesis phase via mage-claude-orchestrator.\n\n**Current state**: No generated code on the working branch (code lives at generation-merged tags). Specs are complete for rel01.0–rel02.1.\n\n**Active release**: rel01.0 — one use case: `rel01.0-uc001-ts` (status: in_progress).\n\n**Existing open issues**:\n- `generation-2026-02-26-23-16-32-dtl`: Implement pkg/testutils (prd001 R1-R5) — **open, do not duplicate**\n\n**Next priority**: After pkg/testutils, cmd/ts implementation is the next dependency in rel01.0. cmd/ts has no pkg/sys or pkg/format dependency per the dependency map — it uses only stdlib. This task can be executed in parallel with or after the pkg/testutils task since the implementation does not depend on it (only the test task will).\n\nProposing one task: cmd/ts implementation (prd004-ts R1-R5, R7-R8), targeting ~200-220 lines. prd004-ts R6 (-r mode, ~60 additional lines) and R9 (differential tests) are deferred to future sessions to stay within the 250-line budget.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/ts timestamp stdin lines core modes (prd004-ts R1-R5, R7-R8)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml (full ts specification)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.0.yaml (test cases covering all modes)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.0-uc001-ts.yaml (end-to-end use case flow)\n      - go.mod (module path github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Full ts implementation -- flag parsing, stdin reading loop, default/custom/subsecond/incremental/elapsed/monotonic modes. Excludes -r mode (prd004-ts R6), which is deferred to a follow-up task.\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default timestamp mode (prd004-ts R1.1-R1.6): read stdin line by line using bufio.Scanner, prepend a strftime-formatted wall-clock timestamp and a single space to each line, flush stdout after each line via bufio.Writer, pass through partial lines at EOF (no trailing newline added), exit 0 on clean EOF. The default format string is '%b %d %H:%M:%S'.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement custom strftime format string support (prd004-ts R2.1-R2.4): accept an optional positional argument containing a strftime format string. Convert strftime percent-specifiers to Go time.Format reference-time tokens (%Y->2006, %m->01, %d->02, %H->15, %M->04, %S->05, %b->Jan, %B->January, %A->Monday, %a->Mon, %Z->MST, %z->-0700, %j->002, %p->PM, %e->_2, unknown specifiers passed through unchanged). Support the ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S (seconds.microseconds e.g. '32.001234'), %.s (unix-epoch.microseconds e.g. '1708358732.001234'), %.T (HH:MM:SS.microseconds e.g. '14:05:32.001234') by detecting and substituting them before the strftime conversion pass, using a single time.Now() call per line for both second and microsecond components.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement -i incremental mode (prd004-ts R3.1-R3.4): timestamp shows elapsed time since the previous line was received (first line shows elapsed since ts started). Default format '%H:%M:%S'. Format elapsed duration using time.Unix(int64(d.Seconds()), 0).UTC().Format(goFmt) to match strftime(fmt, gmtime(delta)) Perl source behavior. -i and -s are mutually exclusive -- if both are given, print a usage message to stderr and exit non-zero.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement -s elapsed-since-start mode (prd004-ts R4.1-R4.3): timestamp shows monotonically increasing elapsed time since ts started, using the same duration-as-UTC-wall-clock formatting as -i. Default format '%H:%M:%S'. A custom format argument overrides the default.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement -m monotonic clock mode (prd004-ts R5.1-R5.3): use the monotonic reading embedded in time.Now() for all timestamp sampling. Compatible with default mode, -i, -s, and all custom format strings including subsecond extensions. When -m is active, the timestamp source must not jump on wall-clock adjustments.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement exit codes and SIGPIPE handling (prd004-ts R7.1-R7.2): exit 0 on clean EOF; exit non-zero and print a usage message to stderr when an unrecognized flag is given. Call signal.Ignore(syscall.SIGPIPE) at program startup. After each write to stdout, if the error is a broken-pipe error (syscall.EPIPE or io.ErrClosedPipe), exit 0 silently without printing an error message. Do not import pkg/sys -- ts has no pkg/sys dependency per the dependency map.\"\n      - id: R7\n        text: \"Implement TZ environment handling (prd004-ts R8.1-R8.2): for wall-clock timestamp modes (default and custom format without -i/-s), resolve the local timezone from the TZ environment variable using time.LoadLocation(os.Getenv('TZ')); if TZ is unset or invalid, fall back to time.Local. For -i and -s elapsed modes, always use time.UTC for formatting regardless of the TZ environment variable.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"strftime-to-Go conversion: implement a convertFormat(strftimeFmt string) (goFmt string, hasSubsecond bool, subsecondKind string) function that first scans the format string for the three subsecond extensions (%.S, %.s, %.T), replaces them with unique sentinel tokens, then walks character by character replacing known strftime specifiers with Go reference-time tokens, and returns the converted format plus metadata about subsecond handling.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Subsecond extension substitution: after formatting the wall-clock or elapsed time via time.Format, do a second pass replacing the sentinel tokens for %.S/%.s/%.T with the computed microsecond strings derived from the same time.Now() sample captured at the start of each line's processing.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Elapsed-time formatting for -i and -s: compute duration d as time.Duration, then format using time.Unix(int64(d.Seconds()), 0).UTC().Format(goFmt). This matches the Perl source behavior of calling strftime(fmt, gmtime(int(delta))) with TZ=GMT, so that a 5-second delta produces '00:00:05' with the default '%H:%M:%S' format.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"The binary is package main under cmd/ts/. The go.mod module is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils. Use os/signal, syscall, bufio, fmt, os, strings, time -- all stdlib. No external or internal package imports are needed for this implementation. Flag parsing uses the standard flag package; the positional format argument is retrieved via flag.Args() after flag.Parse().\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"'go build ./cmd/ts/' succeeds and produces a binary. 'golangci-lint run ./cmd/ts/' reports zero warnings or errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"printf 'line1\\\\nline2\\\\nline3\\\\n' | ./bin/ts produces exactly three output lines, each starting with a timestamp matching the pattern of '%b %d %H:%M:%S' (e.g. 'Feb 27 15:04:05 line1') followed by a single space and the original line content.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | ./bin/ts -s produces three lines with HH:MM:SS elapsed timestamps that are monotonically non-decreasing (each timestamp >= the previous one).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"printf 'first\\\\nsecond\\\\nthird\\\\n' | ./bin/ts -i produces three lines, each with an HH:MM:SS incremental timestamp showing time since the previous line.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"./bin/ts -i -s exits non-zero and writes a usage message to stderr. stdout must be empty.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"./bin/ts -z exits non-zero and writes a usage message to stderr (unrecognized flag).\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"printf '' | ./bin/ts (empty stdin, immediate EOF) produces no output on stdout, no output on stderr, and exits 0.\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"printf 'partial' | ./bin/ts (stdin with no trailing newline) produces exactly one output line prefixed with a timestamp and space, with no extra newline appended beyond what was in the input.\"\n      - id: AC9\n        text: \"printf 'tick\\\\n' | ./bin/ts '%.S' produces one line with a 'seconds.microseconds' prefix (e.g. '1740672000.001234 tick') where the microseconds field has exactly 6 decimal digits.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73796,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73796},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f42cd5a-1743-418a-a045-f5a4dc0bd662","uuid":"0186f6e9-6634-4336-bd14-703e1e667fa7"}
